            <ArticleTitle>Experience with transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation for relief of intractable pain in cancer patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>203-13</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Sixty patients with intractable cancer pain were subjected to transcutaneous electrical stimulation for pain control. Evaluation, after two weeks of treatment, revealed: 17 (28.3%) excellent response, 22 (36.2%) fair and 21 (35.0%) no relief. Re-assessment after 3 months revealed 9 (15%) excellent responses, 11 (18.3%) fair and 40 (67%) failures. Extremity and trunk pains appeared to be most rewarding to patient pain, so far as pain relief is concerned. Perineal and pelvic pains were most difficult to control, only 5 of 12 (41%) cases obtained some short term relief. Pain location and sources correlated with treatment results.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
